   "body": "**Adversarial review (Opus 5.5) — REQUEST CHANGES** — head 03c00c34 (branch fix/587-refresh-after-zoom, before the PR)\n\nThe cause and the formula are right. Scroll-view content coordinates are scaled by `zoomScale`, but `contentInset` and `obscuredInsets.top` are screen points and are not scaled. So the content at the bar's edge is `(contentOffset.y + inset)/zoomScale` CSS px, which is exactly what this PR divides. `readingTop` has one caller, `ReaderWebController.swift:133` (a refresh with `keepsScrollPosition`); page turns, link returns, landings and the stored reading position use other paths. The viewport is `initial-scale=1` (`TopicHTMLBuilder.swift:80`).\n\n1. **medium** — `ReaderZoomRestoreTests.swift:8-24`: nothing tests that `readingTop` uses `layoutY`. Reverting `ReaderWebController+Scroll.swift:30-31` to the old one-liner keeps every test green, and the red was against a stubbed `layoutY` (LESSONS #2). Fix: a web test in `ReaderInsetsWebTests`: scroll to 1000, `setZoomScale(2, animated: false)`, `show(tallDocument(token: 2, keepsScrollPosition: true))`. Expect `window.scrollY` within ±1 of the CSS content at the bar's edge before the zoom, and `zoomScale == 1` once the new document is ready.\n2. **low** — `ReaderWebController+Scroll.swift:27-28`: \"the next document opens unzoomed\" is unverified and not needed, since `window.scrollTo` takes CSS px at any scale. The PR body's \"would land too high\" is also wrong (it would be off only by `inset·(1−1/zoom)`). Give CSS px as the reason.\n3. **nit** — `ReaderScrollGeometry.swift:26`: the invalid-scale guard covers a state UIKit never produces; keep it or drop it.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
